# Findings – DVWA CSRF

## 1. Vulnerability Summary

| Field             | Details                                |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Vulnerability     | Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)      |
| Application       | Damn Vulnerable Web Application (DVWA) |
| Module            | CSRF                                   |
| Endpoint          | `/DVWA/vulnerabilities/csrf/`          |
| Security Level    | Low                                    |
| Severity          | High                                   |
| Authentication    | Required                               |
| Request Method    | GET                                    |
| Affected Function | Password Change                        |
| Status            | Confirmed in lab                       |

---

## 2. Description

A **Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)** vulnerability occurs when an application performs a sensitive action based primarily on the victim's authenticated session without sufficiently verifying that the request was intentionally initiated by the user.

In the tested DVWA CSRF module, the password-change functionality accepts password parameters through a GET request.

The observed parameters were:

```text
password_new=test1234!
password_conf=test1234!
Change=Change
```

No dedicated unpredictable CSRF token was observed in the request.

---

## 3. Affected Functionality

The vulnerable functionality is:

```text
Change your admin password
```

The application provides:

```text
New password
Confirm new password
Change
```

This is a security-sensitive operation because it modifies authentication credentials.

---

## 4. Observed Request

During testing, Firefox Developer Tools captured the following request structure:

```text
GET /DVWA/vulnerabilities/csrf/?password_new=test1234!&password_conf=test1234!&Change=Change
```

The request returned:

```text
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
```

The request also contained an authenticated DVWA session cookie:

```text
PHPSESSID=<authenticated-session>
security=low
```

